New concept: ord() and chr()
Every character has a numeric code behind the scenes (ASCII). These two functions convert between a character and its number:
ord('a')   # → 97   (character → number)
chr(97)    # → 'a'  (number → character)

Lowercase letters a to z are ASCII codes 97 to 122 — 26 letters in a row.

The shifting trick

To shift a letter forward by n positions and wrap around back to 'a' after 'z' (so shifting 'z' by 1 gives 'a', not something outside the alphabet), we use this formula:
shifted = chr((ord(letter) - 97 + n) % 26 + 97)

Breaking this down:
ord(letter) - 97 → converts the letter to a position 0-25 (a=0, b=1, ... z=25)
+ n → shifts it forward by n positions
% 26 → wraps around if it goes past 25 (back to the start of the alphabet)
+ 97 → converts back to the real ASCII code
chr(...) → converts that number back into a letter

Hint for the full encode function
def encode_letter…
Actually — you haven't learned functions yet (that's Day 31), so write this using a loop that builds up a new string character by character:
message = input("Enter message: ").lower()
shift = int(input("Enter shift: "))

result = ""
for letter in message:
    if letter.isalpha():   # only shift actual letters, leave spaces/punctuation unchanged
        shifted = chr((ord(letter) - 97 + shift) % 26 + 97)
        result += shifted
    else:
        result += letter   # spaces, punctuation stay as-is
print("Encoded:", result)
For decoding
Decoding is just encoding with a negative shift — shift backward instead of forward:
shifted = chr((ord(letter) - 97 - shift) % 26 + 97)
Python's % handles negative numbers correctly here too, so this works even if the subtraction goes below 0.)


